[{:seon.task/name "design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `my.task/update!` edits a member (:seon.task/plan, :seon.task/instructions, :seon.task/proofs, :seon.task/cause) of this agent's own task only (`seon.task/member-tx` takes any task, update! passes `(current)`), and `seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs` carries those four, :seon.task/proofs included, so a design agent's proofs on its own task reach main. Reproduction: a staged request with no proofs (`seon.template/stages`); design writes red deftests and rewrites its plan; implement, a different task, is refused :no-proofs by `seon.reconcile/done-explain` (docs/tasks.md). Cause: no function edits another task's members, so the proofs design writes for implement cannot be put on implement's task. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §5.2, prototyped): ``my.task/edit!`` (id f & args: clojure.core/update on any authored member of any task, written on the branch through `seon.task/member-tx`). Deletes: `my.task/update!`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-test/edit-writes-another-tasks-proofs seon.reconcile-test/implement-holds-the-proofs-design-wrote}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-merges-from-its-data"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"I want to be able to go from a fresh chat session to work being done and merged\", and kinds go (\"look for explicit data or metadata\"). Built on jolt/task-merges and proved in parts (not landed): seon.task/merges? from the task's data, a task merging itself unless a task that needs it works the same branch (a staged template's merge task), replacing the kind set #{:merge :whole}; seon.reconcile's :merging mark for any task judged ready or at its gate; :merged among the outcomes a message wakes, so a message reopens a merged task (its first turn merges main from the commit main merged, f25b21f2f4); seon.reconcile/done-explain's :no-proofs only for a branch that changed something (:changed, the count of program keys changed since its base, from the writer's read and the status's changes). Proofs written: seon.reconcile-test/a-task-merges-when-its-proofs-are-green-whatever-template-made-it, seon.reconcile-test/a-message-reopens-a-merged-task. Withdrawn from landing: it breaks seon.agent-test/a-woken-agent-that-replies-stays-parked (bisected to it, green on main): a woken :user task that only replies is judged :done, :lost or :merged instead of :parked, even with :open restored. Not yet found: the turn loop's merge? is now true for a chat (seon.turn: merge main at a green end, merge-turns, seon.reconcile/hairy); a fix counting merge turns only for a :merge task or unmerged entries did not cure it. Next: rebase onto Context's namespace split (status now in seon/ctx/status.clj), read what the chat's end outcome and its judgment's inputs actually are with that test, fix at the cause; then remove :open by data (a task with no proofs whose branch changed nothing parks). The jolt/land-1108b branch holds the latest state with the test fixes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.task-test/task-merges-itself-unless-task-needs-works-same}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-step-that-prepares-another-task-is-judged-by-a-proof-over-data"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the agent trials, 2026-11-11 (finding 10): a task whose work is another task's plan (a prepare task the work task needs; a refine) has no proof that means anything. Its agent edited the other task's plan by the documented form (docs/tasks.md#an-agent-edits-a-task-its-pull-holds), then ended with a reply and no proof: outcome :parked, so its edit never merged and the task that needs it never started. Cause: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` judges a :design task by its OWN plan changing (:no-design) and a :work task by its proofs; neither fits a step whose output is another task's content. No new kind and no second done rule (docs/data-modeling.md: no kinds): the step's claim is a proof, and the proof reads data. Run at an agent's REPL, red then green: a deftest that reads the agent's own pull (`@seon.ctx/ctx`) and asserts that each task it cites differs from what it was at the agent's base commit (`(my.db/at {:commit (:seon.agent/base-commit c)})`), and, when the step names what to add, that the task now cites it (`seon.task/cites-of`). The agent writes that test on its branch, red first, registers it (`my.task/update! :seon.task/proofs conj`), edits, proves. What must be settled before it can be the standing way: (1) such a test reads the agent's pull, so it is red outside an agent: where it lives (the agent's branch only, in a scratch namespace) and what the merge does with it; (2) `bin/laws catch` judged the agent's own deftest as program (clojure.test's `is` expands to a catch): a test is told by its defining form, a deftest (finding 12, Tests); (3) a verdict of a proof over data must be keyed by the branch's data, or a green is reused after the data moved (finding 14, Tests, asked of the owner). With Optimization for the done rule. When settled, docs/tasks.md gains the section and the prepare and refine templates name the proof."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-preparing-task-is-done-when-the-task-it-cites-changed}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-is-filed-where-its-code-is"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11: seon.ctx/doc-of-a-section-answers-small is filed under seon.ctx and its plan changes two definitions of my.agent. The agent starts in seon.ctx (an agent starts in its task's namespace, the owner's ruling), its proof is assigned to seon.ctx-test, and the example test its status shows is about pattern joins. What exists: a task cites what its plan names (docs/references.md#task-references), so the namespaces of the vars it cites are data. Plan: a problem of the branch that adds or changes a task entry (docs/health.md#what-a-branch-caused): every var the plan cites lives outside the task's namespace and its tests. Closed by moving the entry or by citing what it changes. Not a rule for a task that cites no var. Cost: the task's cites, one seek each. Proof, red first: a task filed under app.report whose plan cites only app.calc/total is one row naming both namespaces; filed under app.calc, none."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-task-filed-away-from-its-code-is-a-row}}
 {:seon.task/name "who-does-a-task-is-a-ref"
  :seon.task/plan "A trial (Point's yes, 2026-11-11), waiting on the owner's choice between the two shapes of a session as an agent. One site branches on the kind :user, the helper's reading: src/seon/reconcile.clj 70 (a task a person does is never done by the judge). And src/seon/task.clj 758 names the set of kinds the gate judges, which follows from this and from the plan rule. No datum says who does a task. If a user is an agent, a task a person does is a task whose agent is that user, and both sites read the agent. Nothing to build until the shapes are chosen; then the kind has no reader left and goes at the next erase."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-review-files-what-a-finished-task-showed"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN part 2 (the owner, 2026-11-11: a review task, then merge; judge and file). What exists: a refine curates its own task on the work's branch (docs/tasks.md#a-refine-curates-its-own-task, seon.task/refine-tx); nothing judges a finished task from outside it. The data, ruled by Data model 2026-11-11 (no new attribute): a review is a task that CITES the work task (as a refine does, never :needs: it is wanted whatever the outcome; its target holds what may be edited, jolt/ctxids), reads the work's session through :seon.task/examples (refs to the work's agent: its first real user), and works on its OWN branch from main, so the tasks it files are new entities that reach main when that branch merges green (seon.task/carry-tx carries only tasks main already holds). Its digest to the owner is a message from its agent to the user. It never edits the reviewed work: an instruction in its plan, not a property (a cited plan is editable at the cite's path). Plan: seon.task/review-decl beside refine-decl and a rule of the reconciler that writes one when a work task merges (or is held), released as the refine is (held after a failure). Deletes nothing; it is the second loop of THE PLAN."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-review-cites-the-work-reads-its-session-and-files-on-its-own-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reflect-fork-curates-its-task-mid-run"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN part 2 (the owner, 2026-11-11: a mid-task reflection). What exists: an agent may fork (seon.system's spawn, :seon.agent/agents holds what it started); a refine runs only at a task's end. The data, ruled by Data model 2026-11-11 (no new ref IF the agent starts it): the working agent starts its own fork with a small task that cites its task; the fork is a new agent in the starter's :seon.agent/agents, its conversation its own, the cut its branch's base (:seon.agent/base-commit). What it leaves: an edit to the task's plan (it reaches main at the run's end by carry-tx) or a message to its parent. Decided here: the agent starts it (a system-started reflection would need a forked-from ref, Data model's item 8 shape A, not built). Plan: a my.task function the agent calls (one form, named on its page at a turn budget's half), and its proof. Deletes nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-reflect-fork-edits-its-parents-plan-and-messages-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tasks-namespace-matches-literally-in-a-query"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Data model. Found 2026-11-12 by Web, in one real agent turn on jolt/debuglabel's own system (deepseek; asked: how many tasks does seon.render have). The model spent 4 of its 10 calls on one query: [?t :seon.task/ns seon.render] in a :where clause matches nothing (a quoted symbol answered :parser/binding or zero rows), and only (= (str ?ns) \"seon.render\") or an :in binding counted the 28. A symbol-valued attribute that a datalog query cannot match literally costs every agent turns. The ordinary way: Datahike matches a constant value in a clause, as Datomic does for strings and keywords. Plan: first read at a REPL why the symbol constant does not match (Datahike's parser, or the value as stored); then either make the clause match (a fix in our Datahike fork, if the parser is the cause) or store the attribute as a value a clause matches, settled by Data model. Proof to write: a query (d/q '[:find (count ?t) . :where [?t :seon.task/ns seon.render]] db) counts the tasks of that namespace."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
